00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.270
And the reason for
that is that when

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:54.780
you have your core applications,
some of them in the Cloud

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:56.610
and some in the data
center, we quickly

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:59.980
realized there is latency
in that communication.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:04.230
So we had to make sure that
our database, our APIs,

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.560
and templates that talked to
it had to be moved at once.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:10.410
And then everything else we
could kind of stagger along

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:13.600
and migrate as we went through.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.550
We also obviously tested it very
well in staging, load tested.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:18.900
That's very important.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.790
Tried to simulate
production traffic,

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.767
it's not easy but do
as much as you can.

00:10:23.767 --> 00:10:25.350
Obviously when you
move to production,

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.270
there are going to
be some gotchas.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.700
I'll actually share one.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:32.130
We have a lot of
traffic to our site,

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:35.820
and what happened to us is
that there is a limit set

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:41.340
on KubeDNS to 150
concurrent connections,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:44.190
and we realized that after
we started migrating.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.750
So hope-- thankfully we
were able to change that,

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:48.330
but that was
definitely something

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.250
that we did not expect.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:53.340
And yeah, moving to staging
and moving to production,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.700
we had to make sure
that we a the plan

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.450
and that we had something
really automated

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:59.580
set up where we can
go from dev staging

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.360
into production with
a click of a button.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.490
Next, one of the first
architectural decisions

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.670
that we had was how do
we set up our projects?

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.650
And we had a couple of
schools of thought there.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.560
One was that we have 50
projects, 50 applications.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.370
Why don't we set up
50 different projects?

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:23.430
We quickly realized that
was difficult for us

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:26.540
and from a security
standpoint was not manageable,

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.960
because now the same developer
was the same person will have

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:33.050
access to both production,
development, and staging

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:37.170
application, which
obviously is not acceptable.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:39.590
The second school of
thought was well then

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:43.100
why don't we just
create one big project

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.350
and have all the
applications there?

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:48.860
Again makes the
management extremely easy,

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:53.810
but concerns again-- security
concerns again that developer

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:55.560
will have access to everything.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.180
So we could not allow that.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.690
So the way that we
structured our projects

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:03.150
was something in the
middle, three projects,

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:06.590
one for development and
staging, one for production,

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:09.360
and one for our
management tools.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.840
So something like
JIRA, Confluence,

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.280
[INAUDIBLE],, we would keep
in our management projects

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:19.040
separately for security
reasons and overall

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:21.220
for easier management.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:25.280
Yeah so that that's how we
decided to approach our project

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.482
architecture.

00:12:27.482 --> 00:12:28.440
WILLIAM ANDERSON: Yeah.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.815
So let's talk a little bit
about how we actually broke up

00:12:30.815 --> 00:12:33.180
the clusters inside of those,
which that diagram already

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.070
began to allude to a little bit.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.020
So in terms of choosing
what really lived

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:40.210
beside other applications
on like a cluster level,

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:42.170
now that we already
had projects defined,

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:45.600
we broke it up into kind of six
main categories, forbes.com,

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.880
which was our varnish
caching layer, all

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.250
of our templates
applications, and our APIs.

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:52.770
As Vadim said, having those
live alongside of each other

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:54.450
was absolutely
necessary, especially

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:57.480
once we started to realize the
latency that was introduced

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.480
between communicating with the
GCP back to our data center

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:03.330
compared to how fast that could
be if everything was just side

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.797
by side.

00:13:04.797 --> 00:13:06.630
The other one is our
ETL pipelines and tools

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:07.740
related to that.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:11.160
We named it Mules, but
it's ActiveMQ and MuleSoft

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:12.867
living alongside each other.

00:13:12.867 --> 00:13:14.700
We have multiple content
management systems,

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.810
some used more than others.

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:19.020
And all of those
ended up living inside

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.080
of another one really
providing access

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.560
to the right layer for
our editors and writers.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:28.170
And that's our authentication
and permissions application

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:31.920
in API, logging, which handles
revision control as well

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.740
as access to content
monitoring, our AI skills

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:38.250
which augment writing
facilities for writers

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:41.070
as they go through
creating content,

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:45.120
and social bots as well for
posting to Twitter, LinkedIn,

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:46.140
et cetera.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:49.440
We also had DAM which handled
thumbor for images, Varnish

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.080
for caching, in our DAM API.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.670
Also with the upside
down smiley face

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.440
is a legacy one,
which I will not

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.660
delve into everything
that is in there.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:13:59.820
Spoilers are that it
is a much longer list

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:01.750
than the other ones.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:03.700
And then finally,
admin or management,

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:05.760
which Vadim talked
about as well, where

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:08.100
we store Jenkins Vault,
monitoring tools, JIRA,

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:11.370
Confluence, and any other
kind of utilities that

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.830
allow us to deliver work or
work towards delivering work

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:14.580
in the day to day.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:16.580
VADIM SUPITSKIY: Well,
and the reason the legacy

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:18.100
is separate is
that at some point,

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:19.600
we hope it is going to go away.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.200
And then this cluster
just doesn't exist, right?

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.680
So we're looking forward
to that day in the future.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:26.180
WILLIAM ANDERSON:
Yeah, it'll slowly

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.820
be migrated out of existence.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:31.910
VADIM SUPITSKIY: And one thing
that we had to really focus on

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:35.680
and was new to us in
the Cloud was security.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:36.710
In data center, we.

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:38.910
Were very comfortable
with our security.

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:40.790
We knew exactly
how to handle it.

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:43.250
Everything was
firewalled nicely.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:46.680
So we had nothing to
worry about at that point,

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:48.740
because we had so much
experience with it.

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:50.930
Starting to move to
the Cloud, we quickly

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:54.460
realized that it's not
going to be that simple.

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:56.740
The reason is, we
move an application,

00:14:56.740 --> 00:15:00.020
it should be accessed-- so
should be accessed immediately,

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.540
available to the world, right?

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:08.120
So we immediately started
to pare the access down.

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:10.820
We had to make sure that
only applications that

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:13.380
needed to talk to each
other had that access.

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:16.910
So our diagram we did in
the beginning of our audit

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:18.240
helped us a lot.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.630
We realized which applications
had to talk to each other,

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:24.960
and we would carefully only
allow that level of access.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:27.350
We also obviously had to
audit all the roles and who

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:30.560
has access to that
project, a management

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:32.330
project or production project.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.900
We left that really a
small number of people

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:39.110
to be able to access to
production and management

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:42.380
projects because most of our
deployments and everything

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:44.960
happens through the
code, so in reality,

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:46.460
unless you really
have to, you don't

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:50.540
need to have access to those
projects by themselves.

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:53.300
And I want to give a shout out
to our security and networking

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:54.180
team.

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:55.610
They've done an amazing job.

00:15:55.610 --> 00:16:00.480
They learned all of this and all
these new tools really quickly.

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.810
We could not have
done it without them.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:03.930
They just jumped on it.

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:07.350
They loved it, and they set
it up really, really well.

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:08.127
So really happy.

00:16:08.127 --> 00:16:09.710
WILLIAM ANDERSON:
The same, and that's

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.900
an amazing advantage that we
really had during all this too.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.400
I realize not every
company actually

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:16.310
has internal networking teams
and internal security teams

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:18.980
to provide assistance with that
and to do auditing throughout,

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:22.970
so having them spin
up their skills in GCP

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:25.990
and apply their pre-existing
knowledge really

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:27.740
just put us in a much
more confident place

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:28.790
throughout the whole process.

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:29.930
VADIM SUPITSKIY: And
learning new technologies,

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:31.888
like [INAUDIBLE] which
we obviously didn't than

00:16:31.888 --> 00:16:34.850
have was critical for
us, like kind of how easy

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:38.420
it makes kind of our networking
and splitting traffic

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:42.665
and things like that was
really amazing for us.

00:16:42.665 --> 00:16:45.290
WILLIAM ANDERSON: So let's talk
a little bit about the refactor

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:48.950
itself, and what changed as
we went from our data center

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:51.720
into GCP.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.580
So what we originally
thought, and this

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.320
makes a tremendous
amount of sense to me,

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:56.987
hopefully it does to
all of you as well,

00:16:56.987 --> 00:16:58.487
is we're going to
evaluate if Google

00:16:58.487 --> 00:16:59.760
has a managed solution, right?

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.770
They have something like
called Data Flow, Dataproc,

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:02.160
like whatever.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:02.940
We're just going to use that.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:03.630
It'll be fantastic.

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:04.838
We don't need to do anything.

00:17:04.838 --> 00:17:05.338
Right?

00:17:05.338 --> 00:17:07.630
If they don't have one for
it, though, that's fine too.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:08.790
We can move it as it is.

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:10.540
We'll just throw it
in a compute instance,

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:11.650
and we'll refactor it later.

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:13.233
The important thing
for us, let's just

00:17:13.233 --> 00:17:14.609
get out of the data center.

00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:18.338
So after that though, yeah.

00:17:18.338 --> 00:17:19.380
It seemed highly logical.

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:20.310
I led with that.

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:21.530
My bad.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:22.099
Yeah.

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:25.829
So, but, if we were going to
containerize, we had already

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:28.410
containerized something, why
don't we orchestrate it, right?

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:29.640
And once we have
it orchestrated,

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.348
well now it makes
sense for us to include

00:17:31.348 --> 00:17:33.738
a network mesh like Istio
into the process too?

00:17:33.738 --> 00:17:36.030
And now that we have all
three of those layers as well,

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.140
and we have 50 services,
what are we going to do?

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:40.440
But we to automate a way
to not only deploy those,

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:44.220
but to move those from dev,
staging prod, do migrations,

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:46.270
do network updatings,
do patching.

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:47.820
And then, of course,
like we can't

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:49.963
write an individual pipeline
for all 50 services.

00:17:49.963 --> 00:17:52.380
So we need to abstract it into
something that can actually

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:53.545
run across all of those.

00:17:53.545 --> 00:17:55.170
So while originally
were like, oh we're

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:56.753
just going to move
them into the Cloud

00:17:56.753 --> 00:17:59.280
and be done with it, very soon
after we got through step one

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.010
we found ourselves going
layer and layer of abstraction

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:05.402
higher as we moved
along with migration.

00:18:05.402 --> 00:18:07.860
And I think one of the biggest
things that kind of came out

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:11.940
of that was a highly
configurable master Jenkins

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:14.762
build that does a lot of
what I talked about before.

00:18:14.762 --> 00:18:16.470
It's highly parameterized,
which actually

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.137
means it requires a
bit of training just

00:18:18.137 --> 00:18:20.170
to know how to build to it.

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.520
You can hit last build too
if you know the service,

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:24.270
but this isn't just
building something out

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:26.580
like a new feature into
production-- non-production

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:29.130
into dev environments,
which is automated

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:30.582
or moving that over to staging.

00:18:30.582 --> 00:18:32.790
This is really for like
setting up our infrastructure

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:34.470
and connections
between new services

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.780
as we move them between
those different layers

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:38.550
or introduce a completely
new application

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:40.018
into our architecture overall.

00:18:40.018 --> 00:18:42.435
VADIM SUPITSKIY: So if you
needed to set up a new cluster,

00:18:42.435 --> 00:18:45.450
, set up node pools,
set up networking,

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:48.610
set up new DNS entries,
everything can be done with

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:51.910
a couple of settings and
the click of a button.

00:18:51.910 --> 00:18:55.410
So this file actually
so crucial for us

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:59.520
that moving from dev to stage,
from stage to production,

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:01.950
or introducing a new
application or new cluster,

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:04.940
or even upgrading the
cluster, which happens a lot,

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:07.020
was a breeze for us.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:10.942
Very, very important to
automate, in my opinion.

00:19:10.942 --> 00:19:11.900
WILLIAM ANDERSON: Yeah.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:13.490
Which landed us
with our new stack.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:15.890
So of course, we've already
talked about Kubernetes

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:16.800
quite a bit.

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:20.370
We also have Google Cloud
Storage, Istio, BigQuery, HELM.

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:22.160
We have Cloud
Functions, currently

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.920
just for billing alerts, but
that's also very important

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:28.340
so please set those up if
you're moving over, as well

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:32.000
as ActiveMQ, MuleSoft, HashiCorp
Vault for password and secret

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:33.450
storage.

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:36.620
MongoDB Atlas is our database
for all environments,

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:39.930
but where we're now
in Atlas, as well as

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:43.680
Elastifile, which replaces
our pre-existing headend.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:45.680
VADIM SUPITSKIY: And
here's our new architecture

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:48.110
diagram in the Cloud.

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:53.330
It's very nicely structured and
clear and clean, in my opinion.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:56.750
So we have one CDB,
Fastly, in front of it.

00:19:56.750 --> 00:19:59.060
No F5 Load Balancer anymore.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:01.580
The cluster is divided
as we discussed before.

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:04.430
Our core cluster has
Varnish templates

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:06.150
and API really
close to each other.

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:08.630
So there is no latency.

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:11.630
We have the DAM digital
asset management cluster,

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:14.460
which has thumbor for
resizing, Varnish for caching,

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.190
and DAM API, our
home-grown solution.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:21.320
We also have our CMS is a single
application living in the cloud

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:22.830
storage.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:26.510
Those AI skills that we
had in [INAUDIBLE] we

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:28.880
migrated pretty easily to GCP.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:32.300
So they have kind of
their own place there.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:34.850
Then ETL cluster
is also separated.

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:40.130
Again, our ActiveMQs and
MuleSoft, MongoDB and Atlas,

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:40.940
huge help.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:45.890
Everything can be done on the
fly there, upgrades, indexes,

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:46.800
whatever you need.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.700
So they don't have to worry
about that, so hosted MongoDB

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:50.510
solution.

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:51.150
WILLIAM ANDERSON:
And if you recall,

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.420
we were MongoDB before
the migration as well,

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:55.430
but we moved over to
Atlas versus hosting

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:57.570
that ourselves as we were
moving into the Cloud.

00:20:57.570 --> 00:21:00.920
VADIM SUPITSKIY: Yeah, and
they offer all three providers,

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:01.820
cloud providers.

00:21:01.820 --> 00:21:03.110
Obviously we're using Google.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:07.620
And one thing that we had to
do, for speed we had to be

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:13.130
peer our applications,
Google Cloud and project

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:18.170
to the actual Atlas
project and Atlas Mongos.

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:20.930
So that was very critical for
us because we did see latency

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:23.330
at first, and then after
we did that and the speed

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.420
was improved tremendously.

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:29.630
The Apache server that we
had in front of our NFS also

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:32.660
in Kubernetes now, and NFS.

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:34.490
We did a lot of
proof of concepts

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:37.370
and we moved it
to Elastifile kind

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:39.830
of a hosted solution for that.

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:41.570
So yeah, obstructed
a little bit,

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:44.328
but that's in a nutshell,
our new architecture.

00:21:44.328 --> 00:21:46.370
WILLIAM ANDERSON: One of
the most powerful things

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:48.787
from this as well is looking
back to our old architecture.

00:21:48.787 --> 00:21:50.510
It was a lot of bespoke
solutions, right?

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:53.660
It was like how things had
evolved over time and patched,

00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:55.740
and part of moving
over to the Cloud,

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:57.740
and that Jenkins build
we talked about before,

00:21:57.740 --> 00:22:00.262
was really taking a moment to
see how we could reconfigure

00:22:00.262 --> 00:22:02.720
things and abstract them at
another level, which also makes

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:05.180
for, to be frank, a much
prettier architecture diagram

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:07.100
to present to conferences too.

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:09.920
VADIM SUPITSKIY: Yeah, and
Kubernetes everywhere, pretty

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:14.790
much, and Istio for networking.

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:15.820
So the learnings.

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:19.540
What did we learn
from our migration?

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:24.780
One thing, we quickly realized
we have to monitor our costs.

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:26.190
Set up alerts there.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:29.080
Make sure that you
really estimate

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:32.100
well of what things are
going to cost you, and make

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.740
sure you have really
rigorous alert set up

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:38.250
so you get notified
if something happens.

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:41.250
We learned really
quickly, in the beginning,

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:45.510
that we accidentally turned on
debug the logging on our Istio

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:46.830
to go to Stackdriver.

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:49.020
And that, of course
spiked, all right.

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:52.380
We luckily managed to
catch it pretty quickly.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:56.290
If it didn't, I don't know how
much it would have cost us.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:58.780
But after that, we set
up alerts really nicely,

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:01.050
and now anything happens,
we get the notification

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:03.683
in Slack and by email that's
something that's going out.

00:23:03.683 --> 00:23:05.850
WILLIAM ANDERSON: And
watching those numbers tick up

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:10.170
in semi-real time for the
alerts was quite an experience

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:11.610
as well until we
turned that off.

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:11.820
Yeah,

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:12.540
VADIM SUPITSKIY: It's exciting.

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:15.260
It's different from being in
a data center where everything

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.220
is kind of static.

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:20.820
Another struggle and challenge
was taking a lot of new tech

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:21.780
at once.

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:25.770
So I guess then we had to learn
a lot of new technologies,

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:29.100
and we didn't want to leave
a lot of technical debt,

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:32.730
so kind of managing that of
taking on a lot of new stuff

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:35.460
like Istio, Hound that we
had no experience before

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:37.890
and also doing things
right was definitely

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:40.290
something we had to manage.

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.720
Team skills growth
vs. a timeline.

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:44.890
Our timeline was
pretty aggressive.

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:47.640
And again, kind of making
sure that the team was

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:51.090
comfortable with new tech, was
comfortable with supporting

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:54.510
cloud, and our infrastructure
in the Cloud vs.

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:57.330
also pushing forward quickly.

00:23:57.330 --> 00:23:59.850
another challenge that we had.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:01.770
And doing a lot
of different POCs.

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:03.600
So one thing that
we learned, and I

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:05.730
think is a great
thing to do is fail

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:08.040
quickly when you do
proof of concept,

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:09.180
and don't get attached.

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:11.670
If something doesn't
work, just move on

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:13.290
and do another proof of concept.

00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:15.193
Find the right solution for you.

00:24:15.193 --> 00:24:16.860
WILLIAM ANDERSON: A
layer of abstraction

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:18.510
added to that too
was we actually

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:21.120
introduced HELM early
on in our process,

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:23.040
but not at the very beginning.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:24.595
And it came onto
our radar shortly

00:24:24.595 --> 00:24:26.720
after we started and already
gotten some work done.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:28.950
And the same kind of applies
to the inverse of proof

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:31.350
of concepts if you've already
started down one road,

00:24:31.350 --> 00:24:32.500
and then you discover
something you're like,

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:34.530
actually this is just going
to abstract it on a completely

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:35.110
different level.

00:24:35.110 --> 00:24:36.610
So we don't have
to worry about it.

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:39.570
Looking at the gains of making
that switch versus doubling

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:41.647
down on the road that
you've already taken

00:24:41.647 --> 00:24:43.980
can actually save a tremendous
amount of time and effort

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:45.113
in the long run too.

00:24:45.113 --> 00:24:47.280
VADIM SUPITSKIY: Yeah, and
the last point is balance

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:50.460
and moving quickly manually
and building automation

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:52.180
was really critical for us.

00:24:52.180 --> 00:24:54.858
So obviously, the urge
is to move quickly.

00:24:54.858 --> 00:24:56.400
And what we started
to do, we started

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:57.690
to just move things over.

00:24:57.690 --> 00:24:58.440
It was Kubernetes.

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:00.150
But a lot of the
things were set up

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:04.890
manually like clusters so that
manual upgrades were manual,

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:08.130
a lot of builds of a Jenkins,
but kind of setting up

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:09.870
security, set up networking.

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:12.130
DNS was done manually.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:15.690
And we would realize every time
it's like, when we do something

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:17.160
why don't we automate it?

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:21.750
So we would take a step forward,
take a couple of steps back,

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:24.720
and we would do that a lot
actually, at least a few times

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.427
for each.

00:25:26.427 --> 00:25:27.510
But I think it's worth it.

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:28.927
I think if one
thing that they had

00:25:28.927 --> 00:25:32.400
to change, I would try
to build that foundation

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:35.230
and build automation
from the get go.

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:38.142
It's hard to know that in the
beginning, before you actually

00:25:38.142 --> 00:25:40.600
commit to because every time
you do something, you're like,

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:41.670
oh OK.

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:44.680
We're doing it manually, why
don't we go back automated?

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:47.400
And at the end,
where we came to is

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:49.950
we can do have all of that
with some configuration

00:25:49.950 --> 00:25:51.220
and the click of a button.

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:52.770
WILLIAM ANDERSON: And you might
feel like you're losing time

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:54.510
by taking those backstops,
but especially when

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:56.718
we get in the crunch towards
the end of our timeline,

00:25:56.718 --> 00:25:59.160
and it just became choosing a
couple options from dropdown

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:02.190
menus in Jenkins versus
having to do like actual work

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:04.532
to move things along,
it paid dividends.

00:26:04.532 --> 00:26:06.240
VADIM SUPITSKIY:
Definitely wouldn't help

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:09.970
you learn that pretty quickly.

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:11.850
And now the benefits, right?

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:14.460
Obviously speed to market.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:16.530
What it helps us
with was our kind

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:18.840
of process of
deployment, process

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:21.390
of approvals with our
stakeholders and product

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:22.020
owners.

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:25.950
Now we can easily, when
we have a feature, create

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:30.360
a box in which we can
send out all stakeholders

00:26:30.360 --> 00:26:34.180
or anybody in the company
to take a look, approve it,

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:37.470
and then we can push
it to production.

00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:39.280
Obviously it
reduces manual work.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:41.610
Now our ops and dev
upstream doesn't

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:43.800
need to do manual patches.

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:47.143
They don't have to go to the
data center, and I don't know--

00:26:47.143 --> 00:26:48.560
WILLIAM ANDERSON:
Restart servers.

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:50.670
VADIM SUPITSKIY:
--restart servers.

00:26:50.670 --> 00:26:54.120
Not-- none of that is needed
to be handled by us now.

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:57.400
That's all handled by Google,
which we are very happy about.

00:26:57.400 --> 00:26:59.910
CI/CD pipelines,
obviously much easier.

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:03.630
Again, generated those
boxes and environments

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:06.070
on the fly for each
application, for each feature,

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:11.360
so they can be reviewed by
stakeholders or QA, critical.

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:14.310
Canary deployment,
so basically pushing

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:18.570
some percentage of the
traffic to the new release.

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:21.300
And then, if it goes
well, push it to 100%.

00:27:21.300 --> 00:27:22.980
If not, just scale down.

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:24.600
Destroy the box.

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:26.940
That is really big
for us for our kind

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:30.570
of stability and scalability.

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:31.590
And A/B testing.

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:35.490
A/B testing is something that
is crucial to our company.

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.460
We base most of our
major decisions,

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:42.240
or pretty much all our
decisions on, KPIs.

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:43.770
So what we need to
do we need to AB

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:48.180
test every main feature that we
release, and being in the Cloud

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:49.270
makes it much easier.

00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:53.700
We can spin up new environment,
point some traffic to it,

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:57.930
then collect the data,
and see how it performs.

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:00.060
And then scalability, all right?

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:04.260
If we need to process a lot
of data, for example, at once,

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:05.680
we can just scale up.

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:06.330
And that's it.

00:28:06.330 --> 00:28:08.640
Before that, what we
had to do is basically

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:12.110
over provision or servers and
have huge machines in a data

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:15.810
center that basically were
ideal 90% of the time,

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:18.020
and only were needed
maybe like, I don't know,

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:19.470
a couple of days a year.

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:21.960
So it was a waste,
basically just

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.210
to prepare for some
huge traffic spikes.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:28.950
Now it's all scalable
and automated and Yeah.

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:30.810
We just-- basically
what we used to do

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.628
is have huge boxes sitting
there for no reason.

00:28:33.628 --> 00:28:35.670
WILLIAM ANDERSON: On the
note of scalability too,

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:37.620
part of that for us in
our particular industry

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:40.920
is also unpredictability, right?

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:43.950
At Forbes, we really share the
stories of people's success,

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:46.368
and some of those people
are very successful.

00:28:46.368 --> 00:28:48.660
So when they tweet about an
article or something that's

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:51.810
going on Forbes, that can
generate a pretty tremendous

00:28:51.810 --> 00:28:55.422
spike in traffic going to
particular pages or services.

00:28:55.422 --> 00:28:57.630
Another thing is we started
to move faster and faster

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.120
through all of this
as well was also being

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:01.530
able to scale Jenkins, right?

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:05.077
And getting that
into K-8s, and really

00:29:05.077 --> 00:29:06.660
what it was is once
we started to have

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:11.010
a proper pipeline with
boxes built on every pull

00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:13.397
request for product
owner approval,

00:29:13.397 --> 00:29:15.480
we hit bottlenecks very
quickly when we were still

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:16.440
in the data center.

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:18.150
But moving that
over and having that

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:20.920
be abstracted a layer away,
just removed all those worries.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:22.920
VADIM SUPITSKIY: Yeah,
but definitely one thing,

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:26.172
make sure that all those boxes
they're not laying around,

00:29:26.172 --> 00:29:27.630
and that you actually
destroy them.

00:29:27.630 --> 00:29:29.520
So I guess that set
up some automation

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:33.450
that bugs get basically
killed every time

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:34.560
you don't need it anymore.

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.270
The way for assets
works is basically

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:38.460
when a developer
creates a pull request,

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:41.940
the dev box gets created,
and when it's merged

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:43.530
it basically gets destroyed.

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:47.132
So, that way we manage it
and don't just create a--

00:29:47.132 --> 00:29:48.840
WILLIAM ANDERSON: An
infinite number of--

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:52.870
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:53.460
Yeah.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:55.020
So, soon to be
integrated or what

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:56.940
we're really looking
towards for the future,

00:29:56.940 --> 00:30:00.690
now that we're already in
GCP and cut over looking

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:02.667
to expand that even more.

00:30:02.667 --> 00:30:04.500
So like I said, we're
using Cloud Functions.

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:06.180
But right now we're using
them for billing alerts.

00:30:06.180 --> 00:30:08.160
There's definitely
more potential there.

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:09.720
There's definitely some
things to be solved along

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:11.262
the way, in terms
of code management,

00:30:11.262 --> 00:30:14.220
deployments, and how we actually
handle that with our team

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:18.000
and at scale, but
increasingly so more and more.

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:21.510
Cloud AutoML, machine learning
and AI is major to Forbes

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:24.450
as it is to a tremendous
amount of companies right now.

00:30:24.450 --> 00:30:27.180
For us it particularly ties
into editorial assistance

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:29.960
and how we work
with our writers,

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:32.520
though it definitely bleeds
into a number of other parts

00:30:32.520 --> 00:30:34.360
of our organization as well.

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:36.943
VADIM SUPITSKIY: It's the skills
that we showed you basically,

00:30:36.943 --> 00:30:39.570
and a lot of them are based
on using some of these Google

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:42.030
technologies that are
amazing, and we're really

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:44.100
happy to have those
APIs available to us

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:47.640
so we don't have to actually
write our own NLP or sentiment

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:49.090
analysis and things like that.

00:30:49.090 --> 00:30:52.110
So using that a
lot, but as new ones

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:54.520
get kind of created
all the time,

00:30:54.520 --> 00:30:56.353
we would like to expand
as much as possible.

00:30:56.353 --> 00:30:57.937
WILLIAM ANDERSON:
For sure, and adding

00:30:57.937 --> 00:31:00.660
that layer of abstraction helps
our team move with increased

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:01.830
velocity as well.

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:03.840
So Cloud Dataflow
actually is similar

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:05.582
vein for our ETL
pipelines and looking

00:31:05.582 --> 00:31:07.290
to see how that might
be able to abstract

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:09.373
some of the more manual
work that we do right now.

00:31:09.373 --> 00:31:13.890
Manual being manual coding,
not humans doing work.

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:16.227
Cloud Pub/Sub to a similar
but abstracted effect.

00:31:16.227 --> 00:31:18.060
And I think the real
next step for us is now

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:21.510
that we have this whole
architecture migrated over

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:23.880
to Google Cloud is setting
it up with Terraform

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:26.980
so that not only can we set
